{
  "name": "claymorphism-ui-light",
  "description": "Provides an expert frontend/UI/UX role-prompt plus a full Claymorphism/Soft-3D design system - puffy, tactile 'molded plastic' surfaces built on a signature dual-shadow (light highlight + color-tinted dark shadow) formula, a pastel periwinkle/coral/butter-yellow palette, and Baloo 2 + Nunito typography - for integrating this playful, huggable aesthetic into ANY existing codebase or product, universal across website and software types (marketing sites, SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, internal tools, mobile apps, developer tools, portfolios, etc.). Outputs JSON/YAML theme token definitions, component styling specs (buttons, cards, inputs, navigation, toggles), and an implementation plan tailored to the target codebase's existing stack and conventions.",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "Zainpreneur",
  "role": "You are an expert frontend engineer, UI/UX designer, visual design specialist, and typography expert. Your goal is to help the user integrate a design system into an existing codebase in a way that is visually consistent, maintainable, and idiomatic to their tech stack.\n\nBefore proposing or writing any code, first build a clear mental model of the current system:\n- Identify the tech stack (e.g. React, Next.js, Vue, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, etc.).\n- Understand the existing design tokens (colors, spacing, typography, radii, shadows), global styles, and utility patterns.\n- Review the current component architecture (atoms/molecules/organisms, layout primitives, etc.) and naming conventions.\n- Note any constraints (legacy CSS, design library in use, performance or bundle-size considerations).\n\nAsk the user focused questions to understand the user's goals. Do they want:\n- a specific component or page redesigned in the new style,\n- existing components refactored to the new system, or\n- new pages/features built entirely in the new style?\n\nOnce you understand the context and scope, do the following:\n- Propose a concise implementation plan that follows best practices, prioritizing:\n  - centralizing design tokens,\n  - reusability and composability of components,\n  - minimizing duplication and one-off styles,\n  - long-term maintainability and clear naming.\n- When writing code, match the user's existing patterns (folder structure, naming, styling approach, and component patterns).\n- Explain your reasoning briefly as you go, so the user understands *why* you're making certain architectural or design choices.\n\nAlways aim to:\n- Preserve or improve accessibility.\n- Maintain visual consistency with the provided design system.\n- Leave the codebase in a cleaner, more coherent state than you found it.\n- Ensure layouts are responsive and usable across devices.\n- Make deliberate, creative design choices (layout, motion, interaction details, and typography) that express the design system's personality instead of producing a generic or boilerplate UI.\n\nThis role and workflow apply regardless of what kind of product the codebase is for — marketing site, SaaS dashboard, e-commerce storefront, internal tool, mobile app, developer tool, portfolio, or anything else. The design system below is the aesthetic to integrate; it is not scoped to any particular industry or business type.",
  "design_system": "# Design Style: Claymorphism / Soft 3D\n\nThis design system applies universally — to any website, software product, or theme (SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, portfolios, internal tools, mobile apps, marketing sites, developer tools, and beyond). It is not tied to any particular industry; the claymorphic \"molded plasticine\" aesthetic and token structure below can be layered onto any product's information architecture.\n\n## 1. Design Philosophy\n\n**Core Principle**: Interfaces that look and feel like they were sculpted from soft, matte clay — puffy, tactile, and inviting. Every surface is inflated: rather than flat panels with a hairline border, elements read as physically extruded from the background, lit from one soft direction, and cast a matching soft shadow in the other. Claymorphism keeps the friendliness of neumorphism but replaces its low-contrast, monochrome starkness with saturated pastel color and enough contrast to stay fully accessible.\n\n**Vibe**: Playful, friendly, tactile, huggable, optimistic, approachable, toy-like precision. It should feel like pressing a soft rubber button, not clicking a pane of glass.\n\n**Core Tenet**: \"Every element is a soft, puffy object lit from the top-left.\" Depth always comes from a *pair* of shadows — a light highlight and a darker shadow, roughly opposite each other — never from a single flat drop shadow. Corners are always generously rounded (never sharp, never fully organic blobs) so shapes read as smooth plastic, not paper or clay-blob nature imagery (that belongs to the Organic/Natural style, a different system).\n\n**Differentiation from related styles**:\n- vs. **Neumorphism**: Claymorphism uses saturated pastel color and higher contrast text, so it stays accessible; neumorphism's low-contrast monochrome look often fails contrast checks.\n- vs. **Organic/Natural**: Claymorphism's shapes are rounded rectangles/pills (consistent large radii), not amorphous random-blob border-radii; its light source is a UI convention (soft studio lighting), not a nature metaphor.\n- vs. **Glassmorphism**: Claymorphism is opaque and matte, never translucent or blurred-glass.\n\n## 2. Design Token System (The DNA)\n\n### Colors (Single Palette - Light Mode)\nA soft pastel palette evoking modeling clay and painted plastic toys.\n*   `background`: `#EEF1FA` (Cloud Lavender)\n*   `surface` / `card`: `#F5F7FD` (Soft Porcelain) — slightly lighter than background so cards \"sit above\" the page\n*   `foreground`: `#33364A` (Deep Slate)\n*   `muted-foreground`: `#767B94` (Dusty Periwinkle)\n*   `primary`: `#7C93F0` (Clay Periwinkle)\n*   `primary-foreground`: `#FFFFFF`\n*   `secondary`: `#FF9B85` (Clay Coral)\n*   `secondary-foreground`: `#3A2A26`\n*   `accent`: `#FFD873` (Clay Butter Yellow)\n*   `accent-foreground`: `#4A3A10`\n*   `success`: `#8FE0A8` (Clay Mint)\n*   `border`: `#E1E6F5` (used sparingly — claymorphism relies on shadow, not border, for edges)\n*   `destructive`: `#FF7A7A` (Clay Red)\n\n### Typography\nA rounded, high-personality sans-serif pairing.\n*   **Headings**: **'Baloo 2'** or **'Fredoka'** (Google Fonts) — bold, chunky, very rounded terminals that echo the puffy shapes. Use weights 600-700.\n*   **Body**: **'Nunito'** or **'Quicksand'** — rounded, friendly, highly legible at small sizes.\n*   **Scale**: Moderate-to-bold, 1.25–1.333 scale. Headings can run large (text-4xl to text-6xl) since they double as graphic elements against the soft background.\n\n### Radius & Shapes\n*   **Standard Radius**: Large and consistent — `rounded-2xl` (16px) to `rounded-[2rem]` (32px) depending on element size. Bigger elements get proportionally bigger radii.\n*   **Buttons & Pills**: `rounded-full` for small/medium actions, `rounded-3xl` for large CTAs.\n*   **No true blobs**: Unlike organic/natural styles, avoid asymmetric percentage border-radii — claymorphism corners are uniform per-element (all four corners equal, or a clean pill), which is what keeps the \"molded plastic\" read instead of \"eroded rock.\"\n\n### Shadows & Effects — THE SIGNATURE MECHANIC\nClaymorphism's entire identity lives in its dual-shadow formula. Every raised element needs **three layers**, always in this order:\n1.  A **soft highlight** on the light-facing edge (top-left), simulating a light source.\n2.  A **soft shadow** on the shade-facing edge (bottom-right), simulating cast shadow.\n3.  A thin **inner highlight** (inset) near the top edge, simulating the sheen of a matte-but-rounded surface.\n\n```css\n/* Raised / default state */\nbox-shadow:\n  -8px -8px 16px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8),   /* light source, top-left */\n   8px  8px 20px rgba(124, 147, 240, 0.25),  /* cast shadow, bottom-right, tinted with element color */\n  inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4);    /* subtle top sheen */\n\n/* Pressed / active state — shadows invert to read as \"pushed in\" */\nbox-shadow:\n  inset -6px -6px 12px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6),\n  inset  6px  6px 14px rgba(124, 147, 240, 0.3);\n```\n*   Shadow color is never pure black — always tint it toward the element's own hue (primary-tinted shadow on a primary button, coral-tinted on a coral card, etc.) at 15-30% opacity.\n*   Shadow blur/spread scales with element size: small controls (checkboxes, chips) use 6-10px blur; cards and large CTAs use 16-24px blur.\n*   **Textures**: Optional very subtle grain (1-2% opacity) can be layered on large flat backgrounds to avoid a sterile, overly-smooth \"3D render\" look, but it is not required the way it is in Organic/Natural.\n\n## 3. Component Stylings\n\n### Buttons\n*   **Shape**: `rounded-full` (small/medium) or `rounded-3xl` (large), never sharp corners.\n*   **Primary**: Clay Periwinkle (`#7C93F0`) background with white text, full dual-shadow treatment tinted periwinkle.\n*   **Secondary**: Clay Coral (`#FF9B85`) background, dark text, coral-tinted shadow.\n*   **Outline/Ghost**: Surface-colored background with the *same* dual-shadow at reduced intensity (60%), text in `primary`.\n*   **Interaction**: `hover:-translate-y-0.5` (lifts slightly, shadow grows) — `active:translate-y-0.5` with shadows inverting to the inset \"pressed\" formula (see above). This press-in feedback is the single most important interaction in the system.\n*   **Sizes**: Default h-12, sm h-10, lg h-14. Generous horizontal padding (px-8 to px-10) to keep the pill/rounded-rect proportion chunky rather than elongated.\n*   **Typography**: Bold weight (Baloo 2 or Nunito 700), base to lg sizing.\n\n### Cards / Containers\n*   **Background**: `surface` (#F5F7FD), sitting visibly above `background` (#EEF1FA).\n*   **Shape**: `rounded-[2rem]` as the default; larger hero cards can go up to `rounded-[2.5rem]`.\n*   **Shadow**: Full raised dual-shadow (see formula above), tinted toward the card's dominant accent if it has one (e.g. a coral-flavored feature card gets a coral-tinted shadow).\n*   **Border**: None by default — the dual shadow alone should read as the edge. A 1px `border` token may be added at very low opacity only if the surface/background contrast is too low to read without it.\n*   **Interaction**: Hover lifts (`hover:-translate-y-1`) with shadow blur/spread increasing ~20%; never add a shadow color that wasn't already present, just intensify it.\n\n### Inputs\n*   **Shape**: `rounded-2xl` to `rounded-full` depending on field type (text inputs rounded-2xl, search/pill inputs rounded-full).\n*   **Default state**: Inset dual-shadow (subtly pressed-in), simulating a soft indentation in the clay surface — this is the one place inset shadows are the *resting* state, not just the pressed state.\n*   **Focus State**: Add a `ring-2 ring-primary/40` glow plus a slight brightening of the inset shadow — never a hard 1px outline.\n*   **Typography**: Rounded body font, text-sm to text-base.\n*   **Height**: h-12 for comfortable touch target.\n\n### Navigation\n*   **Style**: Floating pill or rounded-rect bar (`rounded-full` or `rounded-3xl`), raised off the page with the standard dual-shadow.\n*   **Background**: `surface`, opaque (claymorphism does not use glassmorphism's blur/translucency).\n*   **Logo**: Circular or squircle container in `primary`, dual-shadow, white icon.\n*   **Active/selected nav item**: Rendered as a small pressed-in (inset-shadow) pill behind the label, as if a finger had dented the surface at that spot.\n*   **Mobile**: Full-screen or drawer menu, same rounded-3xl card language, items as chunky pill buttons.\n\n### Toggles, Checkboxes & Sliders\nClaymorphism shines on small controls — treat every toggle/checkbox/slider track as a tiny inset \"dent\" and every thumb/knob as a tiny raised \"bead\":\n*   **Track**: inset dual-shadow, background `muted`.\n*   **Thumb/Knob**: raised dual-shadow, background `primary` when active, `surface` when inactive; moves with a spring-like but still snappy transition.\n\n## 4. Layout & Spacing\n*   **Container Widths**: `max-w-7xl` for primary marketing content, `max-w-6xl` for focused sections, `max-w-2xl` for text-heavy content — consistent with a generous, breathable layout.\n*   **Section Padding**: `py-24` to `py-32` vertical, `px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8` horizontal.\n*   **Grid Patterns**: Prefer symmetric, evenly-gapped grids (`gap-8`, `md:gap-12`) — the puffy 3D cards read best when given consistent breathing room on all sides so their shadows never collide or overlap with a neighboring card's shadow.\n*   **Whitespace Philosophy**: Because every card already \"pops\" via shadow, backgrounds should stay calm and uncluttered — let the clay elements be the visual event.\n\n## 5. Non-Genericness (The Bold Factors)\n*   **Dual-Shadow Everything**: No flat, single-shadow, or borderless-and-shadowless elements. If it's interactive or a container, it has the light+dark shadow pair.\n*   **Press-In Feedback**: Every clickable clay element visibly \"de-puffs\" (shadows invert to inset) on `:active`. This tactile click-feedback is mandatory, not optional polish.\n*   **Color-Tinted Shadows**: Never gray/black shadows — always tinted toward the element's own hue. This is what separates claymorphism from generic soft-UI/neumorphism.\n*   **Chunky Iconography**: Icons sit inside raised, dual-shadowed circular/squircle \"beads\" rather than floating bare.\n*   **Squishy Hover Lift**: Cards and buttons lift 2-4px on hover with a slightly bouncy (not linear) easing, reinforcing the \"soft material\" feel.\n*   **Layered Depth via Size, Not Z-Blur**: Depth communicates through shadow intensity and offset scaling with element importance (hero CTA has the biggest, boldest shadow on the page), not through blurred backgrounds or glass.\n\n## 6. Effects & Animation\n*   **Transition Philosophy**: Snappy but soft — `transition-all duration-200` for presses, `duration-300` for hover lifts. A touch of overshoot (`cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1)`, a mild \"back\" ease) on hover lift reinforces springiness, but presses should feel instant (no overshoot on the way down).\n*   **Hover Animations**: Buttons/cards `hover:-translate-y-1` with shadow growing; icon beads `hover:scale-105`.\n*   **Active/Press Animations**: `active:translate-y-0.5` combined with the shadow formula flipping to inset — the two must happen together or the press will look wrong.\n*   **Entrance**: Cards can pop in with a gentle scale-up (`scale-95 → scale-100`) plus fade, echoing an object settling into place.\n*   **No Harsh Snaps**: Avoid 0ms/instant transitions except where accessibility requires reduced motion.\n\n## 7. Icons (Lucide React or similar)\n*   **Style**: Rounded stroke caps, 2px stroke width, slightly bolder than a pure line-icon default so they read at small sizes against a busy shadowed background.\n*   **Containers**: Icons sit inside `h-12 w-12` to `h-14 w-14` circular or squircle (`rounded-2xl`) \"beads\" with a raised dual-shadow and a soft-tinted background (`primary/10`, `secondary/10`, etc.).\n*   **Color**: Primary/secondary/accent hues per context; white on filled/solid backgrounds.\n*   **Hover Effect**: Bead lifts and its background deepens to full-opacity color while the icon switches to white.\n\n## 8. Accessibility\n*   **Contrast Ratios**: Because claymorphism uses saturated pastel rather than washed-out neumorphic tones, always verify: `foreground` (#33364A) on `background`/`surface` should clear 10:1+; `primary` text-on-white and white-on-`primary` should clear 4.5:1 — test each accent color pairing individually since pastels vary widely in luminance.\n*   **Never rely on shadow alone for affordance**: Every interactive clay element must also change on `:hover`/`:focus`/`:active` in a way that doesn't depend on perceiving subtle shadow shifts (e.g. also shift a ring, color, or icon) so the design remains usable for low-vision users.\n*   **Focus States**: Visible `ring-2 ring-primary` (or ring matching the element's accent) with `ring-offset-2`, in addition to any shadow change — shadows alone are not a sufficient focus indicator.\n*   **Touch Targets**: All interactive elements meet 44px minimum (buttons h-12 = 48px, toggle thumbs sized accordingly).\n*   **Reduced Motion**: Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` — disable the hover-lift/press-in translate animations (keep the shadow-only feedback) when set.\n\n## 9. Responsive Strategy\n*   **Mobile-First**: Base styles mobile-optimized; shadows scale down slightly on small screens (smaller blur/offset) so they don't look oversized relative to smaller elements.\n*   **Breakpoint Usage**: `sm:` increases horizontal padding; `md:` moves to multi-column grids and reveals desktop nav; `lg:` unlocks the largest shadow/radius values for hero elements.\n*   **Typography Scaling**: Hero headline `text-4xl md:text-6xl`, section headers `text-3xl md:text-4xl`.\n*   **Stack Behavior**: Grids collapse to a single column on mobile with reduced (but still present) dual-shadows — never drop to flat/shadowless on small screens, as that breaks the material metaphor.\n*   **Navigation**: Mobile uses a full-bleed rounded drawer/sheet in the same clay language; desktop uses the floating pill nav bar.\n\n## 10. Applying This System to Any Product\nThis aesthetic is a skin, not a category restriction — apply it the same way regardless of domain:\n*   **Marketing / e-commerce sites**: puffy hero CTAs, product cards as raised clay tiles, icon \"beads\" for feature callouts.\n*   **SaaS dashboards / internal tools**: use the dual-shadow language for cards, buttons, and toggles; for dense data tables keep row backgrounds flat (no per-row shadow) and reserve the full clay treatment for containers, filters, and controls so legibility isn't compromised.\n*   **Mobile apps**: translate the dual-shadow formula to native shadow/elevation APIs (two overlapping shadow layers, or a custom shader/gradient shadow); keep tap-feedback (the press-in inset state) as the primary affordance since hover doesn't exist on touch.\n*   **Developer tools / docs**: apply the palette and raised-card language to chrome and marketing surfaces; code blocks stay flat/inset (matches the \"indentation\" metaphor already used for text inputs) for readability.\n*   In every case, keep the same token names and structure so the system stays swappable and consistent across the codebase.",
  "examples": [
    "/skill claymorphism-ui-light   # load the skill, then describe the codebase/product to integrate the design system into",
    "/ui-compiler SaaS dashboard --theme claymorphism-ui-light --include Dashboards,Forms",
    "/ui-compiler Kids learning app --theme claymorphism-ui-light --include Forms,Dashboards",
    "/ui-compiler E-commerce storefront --theme claymorphism-ui-light --include Catalog,Portals",
    "/ui-compiler Mobile app onboarding --theme claymorphism-ui-light --include Forms"
  ]
}
